(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does griseofulvin reach the site of a dermatophyte infection?","choices":["It is concentrated in sebum and delivered through the sebaceous glands","It is actively transported across the dermis by macrophages","It is applied topically and penetrates the nail plate directly","It is deposited in keratin precursor cells in skin, hair and nails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is why treatment lasts as long as the keratin takes to grow out. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A patient on metronidazole drinks alcohol and becomes flushed and nauseated. What is this?","choices":["Alcohol withdrawal","Serotonin syndrome","Disulfiram-like reaction","Anaphylaxis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The single most testable fact about this drug. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 86"},{"q":"Why is an azole sometimes combined with a corticosteroid?","choices":["For more rapid symptom relief","To prevent the development of resistance","To allow a shorter total course of therapy","To make the preparation less irritating to the skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The steroid addresses symptoms while the antifungal addresses the organism. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How is norepinephrine given?","choices":["By inhalation","As a titrated intravenous infusion","As a single intramuscular injection","Orally, in divided doses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which suits a drug used to support vascular resistance in shock. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A patient presents on day 4 of influenza. What limits oseltamivir?","choices":["The 48-hour window for starting treatment has passed","Treatment should be started at a doubled dose to compensate","Treatment is equally effective at any point in the illness","Acyclovir should be substituted"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because the drug blocks release of new virus rather than clearing existing virus, starting late has little to act on. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which organisms cause the superficial mycoses of hair, nails and mucous membranes?","choices":["Epidermophyton, Microsporum and Trichophyton","Aspergillus, Cryptococcus and Histoplasma","Chromomycosis, Pseudallescheriasis and Sporotrichosis","Candida, Coccidioides and Blastomyces"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These are the dermatophytes that griseofulvin and the allylamines treat. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which features of fungal cells explain why antibacterial antibiotics do not treat them?","choices":["Prokaryotic: peptidoglycan wall, 70S ribosome","No cell wall at all, so wall agents have no target","They replicate only inside host cells, so extracellular antibiotics never reach them","Eukaryotic: chitin wall, ergosterol membrane, nuclear membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every antifungal class in this lecture exploits one of these differences, which is also why selectivity is harder than with bacteria. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which vehicle gives the greatest potency for the same steroid?","choices":["Foam","Ointment","Lotion","Cream"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ointment is greater than cream, which is greater than lotion. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 38"},{"q":"What kind of compound is oseltamivir as administered?","choices":["A monoclonal antibody against viral haemagglutinin","A live attenuated preparation","A prodrug converted to its carboxylate form","An active guanine analogue requiring no conversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — another prodrug in a lecture full of them, alongside valacyclovir, famciclovir and valganciclovir. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which conditions make up the atopic triad?","choices":["Acne vulgaris and rosacea","Asthma and allergic rhino-conjunctivitis","Psoriasis and seborrhoeic dermatitis","Urticaria and angio-oedema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and skin barrier dysfunction is what the lecture says plays the major role in the skin component. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which group of five agents is bacteriostatic?","choices":["Tetracyclines, clindamycin, aminoglycosides, metronidazole, trimethoprim","Erythromycin, tetracyclines, sulfonamides, trimethoprim, clindamycin","Penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, vancomycin, metronidazole","Erythromycin, penicillins, sulfonamides, vancomycin, fluoroquinolones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the five the lecture places on the bacteriostatic side. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which systemic corticosteroid does NOT cause drug-induced acne?","choices":["Hydrocortisone","Prednisone","Dexamethasone","Methylprednisolone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes the eruption is not seen with hydrocortisone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Aminoglycosides are the drug of choice for which situations?","choices":["Clostridium difficile colitis","Febrile neutropenia, sepsis, and enterococcal synergy","Community-acquired pneumonia and acute otitis media","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus skin infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with Enterococcus covered only at synergy dosing rather than as monotherapy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which patient should avoid topical tacrolimus or pimecrolimus?","choices":["A patient using an emollient","A patient with a weakened immune system","A patient with a history of acne","A patient over the age of sixty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture pairs this with a possible cancer risk. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is neostigmine used for?","choices":["Treating an overdose of an anticholinergic drug","Stimulating the bladder and gut, and reversing competitive blockade","Diagnosing myasthenia gravis and assessing cholinesterase therapy","Slowing the progression of Alzheimer disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it can stimulate contractility before paralysing. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Agitation, hallucinations, dilated pupils, dry flushed skin, fever, retention. Which toxidrome?","choices":["Nicotinic, from ganglionic stimulation","Beta blocker overdose","Anticholinergic","Muscarinic, from a cholinergic agonist"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives it as mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet, dry as a bone, hot as Hades, with retention and tachycardia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is timolol's ocular action, and what is it used for?","choices":["It reduces aqueous humour production","It causes miosis, for chronic open-angle glaucoma","It causes mydriasis, for examination of the fundus","It reduces corneal oedema, for keratitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, a different route to lowering pressure than pilocarpine's. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which two conditions does varicella-zoster virus cause?","choices":["Retinitis and colitis","Infectious mononucleosis and hepatitis","Chickenpox and shingles","Cold sores and genital ulceration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — primary infection and later reactivation of the same virus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which pharmacokinetic properties count under drug factors?","choices":["Bactericidal activity, time-dependent killing, and concentration-dependent killing","Absorption, distribution, penetration into body compartments, and elimination","Onset, peak, duration, and half-life","Bioavailability, protein binding, volume of distribution, and clearance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Penetration into compartments is called out separately, which is why central nervous system penetration recurs for individual classes. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does nicotine do at autonomic ganglia?","choices":["It has no ganglionic action and acts only in the central nervous system","It permanently destroys ganglionic receptors","It depolarizes the ganglia, first stimulating and then paralysing them","It competitively and reversibly blocks them from the outset"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the stimulation raises blood pressure, heart rate, peristalsis and secretions before the fall. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How well does ciclopirox nail lacquer work for onychomycosis?","choices":["It is less than twelve per cent effective","It is curative in the majority of patients","It works only on fingernails, not toenails","It is as effective as oral terbinafine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and saying so up front is what keeps expectations honest for a months-long treatment. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which patient should not be given a topical retinoid?","choices":["A patient taking an oral contraceptive","A patient over sixty years of age","A patient who is pregnant","A patient with a history of eczema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says to avoid retinoids during pregnancy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which Bactrim interaction runs through CYP2C9 inhibition?","choices":["Chelation reducing absorption of iron supplements","A significant increase in the international normalised ratio in patients on warfarin","Reduced effectiveness of oral contraceptives through gut flora disruption","Serotonin syndrome with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Other interactions include raised phenytoin, rifampin and digoxin levels, reduced methotrexate clearance, and hypoglycaemia with sulfonylureas. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which adverse reactions are listed for the echinocandins?","choices":["Visual disturbance and hepatic dysfunction","Bone marrow suppression and hepatotoxicity as the principal dose-limiting effects","Renal tubule damage requiring saline hydration","Tachycardia, headache, insomnia, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and blood dyscrasias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Note the electrolyte disturbances overlap with amphotericin B's, despite a completely different mechanism. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which of ganciclovir and valganciclovir is the better-absorbed oral form?","choices":["Valganciclovir, the prodrug","Ganciclovir, the parent compound","Both are equally absorbed","Neither is absorbed orally; both are intravenous only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ganciclovir's oral bioavailability is very low, which is why the prodrug exists. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Succinylcholine with halothane causes rigidity, acidosis, tachycardia and fever. What is the treatment?","choices":["Pralidoxime to reactivate acetylcholinesterase","Atropine to oppose muscarinic overactivity","Neostigmine to reverse the blockade","Rapid cooling and dantrolene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and blocking that release is what reduces heat production. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is the approximate rate of penicillin cross-sensitivity with cephalosporins?","choices":["Approximately 10 percent","Approximately 25 percent","Cross-sensitivity does not occur at all between the two classes","Less than 1 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The figure matters clinically because a reported penicillin allergy does not automatically exclude a cephalosporin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why is propranolol hazardous for a patient prone to hypoglycaemia?","choices":["It raises insulin secretion directly","It increases glucose uptake into muscle","It blocks glucose absorption from the gut","It masks the response to hypoglycaemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it also decreases glycogenolysis and glucagon secretion. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Athlete's foot after prolonged occlusive footwear. Which predisposing factor?","choices":["Loss of barriers","Immunodeficiency","Metabolic abnormality","A warm moist environment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture's own examples for that category are diaper rash and athlete's foot. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A patient is hot, dry, flushed, confused, with dilated pupils and urinary retention. Which toxidrome?","choices":["Sympathomimetic","Opioid","Anticholinergic","Cholinergic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet, dry as a bone, hot as Hades. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 50"},{"q":"What proportion of patients must stop intravenous ganciclovir because of side effects?","choices":["About a third","About 1 in 20","Almost none; it is generally well tolerated","Essentially all patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a striking figure, and the reason the better-absorbed oral prodrug matters clinically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 6-year-old needs an antibiotic. Why avoid doxycycline?","choices":["Teeth discoloration","Tendon rupture","Seizures","Grey discolouration of skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"It binds calcium; also depresses skeletal growth. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 63"},{"q":"What distinguishes ceftriaxone from most other cephalosporins in renal impairment?","choices":["It must be dialysed off before each subsequent dose","It requires no dosage adjustment in renal insufficiency","It requires the largest dose reduction of any cephalosporin in renal insufficiency","It is contraindicated at any degree of renal impairment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is a useful contrast because the class as a whole is mostly renally eliminated. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A patient stopped benzoyl peroxide because of irritation. What is the next step?","choices":["Oral prednisone","Stop all treatment","A topical retinoid","Oral isotretinoin"],"answer":2,"exp":"The next step he gave for this exact scenario. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which receptor does phenylephrine act on, and what happens to the heart rate?","choices":["Alpha-one, with reflex bradycardia","Beta-one, with tachycardia","Beta-two, with no rate change","Alpha-two, with reduced sympathetic outflow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It has no direct cardiac action; the slowing is a baroreceptor reflex. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which mood change must be monitored on isotretinoin?","choices":["Mania","Psychosis","No monitoring is needed","Developing depression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Explicitly flagged for monitoring. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 27"},{"q":"A patient develops red man syndrome during a vancomycin infusion. What is the fix?","choices":["Stop vancomycin permanently","Give adrenaline","Switch to oral vancomycin","Slow the infusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"It is rate-related; the deck infuses 1 gram over 60 minutes. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which vascular effect does propranolol produce, and through which receptor?","choices":["Renal vasodilation, through dopaminergic receptors","Peripheral vasoconstriction, through beta-two blockade","Peripheral vasodilation, through alpha-one blockade","No vascular effect at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, since blocking beta-two removes vasodilation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Why does voriconazole's intravenous formulation require caution in renal failure?","choices":["It crystallises in the renal tubule as acyclovir does","It causes direct renal tubule damage requiring saline hydration","Its vehicle, cyclodextrin, accumulates when renal function is impaired","The drug itself is cleared entirely by glomerular filtration and reaches toxic levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the concern is the vehicle rather than the drug itself, which is an unusual reason to avoid a formulation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"How do the indirect-acting cholinergic agents work?","choices":["They bind muscarinic receptors directly and mimic acetylcholine","They increase synthesis of acetylcholine from acetyl coenzyme A and choline","They block reuptake of acetylcholine into the presynaptic terminal","They increase the activity of acetylcholine by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They raise the transmitter rather than binding the receptor themselves. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinic is approved for axillary hyperhidrosis?","choices":["Glycopyrronium","Dicyclomine","Tiotropium","Scopolamine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, approved in 2018 for that indication. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What happens to cardioselectivity at higher doses?","choices":["It increases","It is unchanged","It converts to alpha blockade","It is lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so a beta-one selective agent can still affect the airway. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"What is the advice about washing in acne?","choices":["Wash often with a high pH soap","Avoid washing the affected area entirely while on treatment","Twice a day only, balancing cleanliness against drying","Wash as often as possible to remove surface oils"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it adds that once rinsed off no active product remains on the skin. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which of the listed antiviral approaches works on the host rather than on the virus?","choices":["Inhibiting viral protein synthesis","Stimulating the host immune system","Inhibiting specific viral enzymes","Blocking viral assembly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is the only one of the eight aimed at the host side of the interaction. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A woman is starting isotretinoin. Which programme must she enrol in?","choices":["REMS for clozapine","A photosensitivity registry","No programme is needed","iPledge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Committed to pregnancy prevention. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 27"},{"q":"How does guanethidine act?","choices":["It blocks transport of transmitters into storage vesicles","It blocks postsynaptic alpha-one receptors","It stimulates presynaptic alpha-two receptors","It blocks release of stored norepinephrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is distinct from reserpine's blockade of vesicular uptake. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"With which agents is flucytosine combined, and for what?","choices":["With voriconazole in invasive aspergillosis, and with fluconazole in vaginal candidiasis","With amphotericin in cryptococcal meningitis","With caspofungin in oesophageal candidiasis, and with terbinafine in onychomycosis","With griseofulvin in tinea capitis, and with nystatin in oral candidiasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is used in combination rather than alone, and its own indications are Cryptococcus neoformans and Candida. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which set gives the MAJOR indicators of atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Raised immunoglobulin E, positive allergy skin tests, and dry patchy scales","Nikolsky sign, mucosal erosion and a preceding fever","Comedones, papulopustular lesions, and nodules","Pruritus, typical distribution, chronicity, family history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Raised immunoglobulin E, positive skin tests, dry patchy scales and infection susceptibility are the minor ones. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why did aminoglycoside dosing move from every 8 hours to every 24 hours?","choices":["Once-daily dosing eliminates the need for therapeutic drug monitoring","Renal clearance is so rapid that more frequent dosing has no effect","Post-antibiotic effect and concentration-dependent killing","Time-dependent killing over a longer interval"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the clearest clinical application of the concentration-dependent pattern from earlier in the lecture. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Voriconazole is derived from which other azole?","choices":["Fluconazole","Itraconazole","Ketoconazole","Clotrimazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a fluconazole derivative introduced in 2002, which then took over systemic aspergillosis from amphotericin B. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Why is polymyxin resistance uncommon, and why is use expected to increase?","choices":["It is used only topically, so systemic resistance cannot emerge","It is always combined with a beta-lactamase inhibitor that prevents resistance","Unused for 50 years; resistance is bringing it back","Bacteria cannot circumvent it and the toxicity is gone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low historical exposure is the reason resistance never developed widely. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"How is rebound congestion from oxymetazoline managed?","choices":["Add an antihistamine","Continue indefinitely","Taper the drug down","Increase the dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"A taper lets the receptors recover. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which three systems make up the autonomic division?","choices":["Enteric, parasympathetic and sympathetic","Somatic, parasympathetic and sympathetic","Afferent, efferent and enteric","Central, peripheral and enteric"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The enteric system is the one most often forgotten of the three. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where are adrenergic receptors located relative to the synapse?","choices":["Presynaptically or postsynaptically","Only postsynaptically on the effector organ","Only presynaptically on the neuron","Only within the central nervous system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and that presynaptic location is what alpha-two agonists exploit. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which three classes make up the cholinergic antagonists?","choices":["Direct-acting agents, indirect-acting agents and reactivators","Alpha blockers, beta blockers and mixed blockers","Choline esters, alkaloids and anticholinesterases","Antimuscarinic agents, ganglionic blockers and neuromuscular blocking agents"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and they are distinguished by which cholinergic site they occupy. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which infection is sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim used for in both treatment and prophylaxis?","choices":["Lyme disease","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","Mycobacterium avium complex","Clostridium difficile colitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside urinary tract infection, bacterial prostatitis, orchitis and epididymitis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which cardiovascular effects does acetylcholine itself have?","choices":["A rise in cardiac output with a fall in heart rate","A decrease in heart rate, cardiac output and blood pressure","An increase in heart rate with a rise in blood pressure","No cardiovascular effect, since its action is confined to glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside increased salivary, intestinal and bronchiolar secretions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Oral candidiasis after broad-spectrum antibiotics. Which predisposing factor?","choices":["Metabolic abnormality","A warm and moist local environment","Suppression of competing organisms","Loss of barriers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Removing the bacterial flora leaves the niche open, which is why antibiotic exposure is itself a fungal risk factor. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which receptors does isoproterenol predominantly stimulate?","choices":["Beta-one and beta-two","Alpha-one and alpha-two","Beta-one only","Alpha-one only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it heads the beta potency order and sits at the bottom of the alpha one. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A corticosteroid is not controlling a plaque. What does the concentration gradient predict?","choices":["Changing to a more drying vehicle will matter more than concentration","Only a change to a systemic agent will overcome it","Raising the concentration increases drug transfer and can overcome the resistance","Concentration has little effect once the stratum corneum is saturated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives corticosteroid resistance overcome by increased concentration as its worked example. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 4"}]};
